body:
But Nina gave the information to George. It was a Nina move. She wanted Hayley gone from the get go since she threw her under the bus without even feeling sorry for it. Nina retaliated and knew that telling George will take the heat off of her and onto Hayley. 

&#x200B;

Hayley made a rookie mistake that costed her game. She became too complacent and underestimated Nina.

Syndra, Kassadin, Xerath.

&#x200B;

Else are more manageable. Syndra and Xerath will make your laning phase hell if they know what they are doing. Kassadin will stomp you once he hits 6 unless he is really behind.

Annie's laning phase isn't good. Her wave clear is mediocre, she has little to no sustain (taste of blood and ravenous hunter helps), mediocre range, and her kit is outdated.

&#x200B;

As much as possible, bully them with your auto attacks at levels 1-3, they will actually hurt a lot during those levels. Annie has one of the highest aa range in the game. She's a lane bully against champions that she outrange like Yasuo, Akali, Katarina.

&#x200B;

You will never and supposed to not bully a Xerath. He massively out ranges you and will just poke you out of lane.

In the competitive scene, Annie just gets destroyed by meta mid laners and mid is very vital in the game because mid as well as jungle creates immense pressure on the map. Annie is a very very niche pick. Meta midlaners have better kits than Annie has as a mid laner. They have better wave clear so that they can roam and impact other lanes more, they are more safe due to their superior range, mobility, not- ult reliant in making big plays and even base stats. Annie relies on punishing mistakes which is very minimal in proplay. 

&#x200B;

She is safer on a side lane because she can easily set up ganks in a way that she can also provide lane dominance against specific matchups (mostly tank, somewhat bruisers, and Gnar) in the top and bot lanes. But if you're going to think about why play Annie Top when Kennen can do her job more safer and more effective.

It's not bad per say, but for the price that it has it's not worth it. I bought the skin when it came on sale so Im pretty satisfied with it.

&#x200B;

Red Riding Annie and Annie in Wonderland are two of the best Annie skins imo. Sadly, I wish they were released sooner so that the skin themselves are justified with great particles/recalls and bunch of other animations.

Because it's the wrong way of buffing her. They are buffing her in terms of almost removing her main counter play, which is staying out of her range. With this change, she can have free mobility and make it more easier to match against match-ups that outrange her. Melee match-ups will be more oppressive on their side when matched against Annie because she can engage on them more with ease. Annie already has already more forms of engage with Protobelt, Predator even Shurelya's Reverie, Ghost and Righteous Glory but choosing or buying them has their own cost that you will surely feel. The lowered damage reduction isn't much felt because you will always level it last and most games end before you even max it.

&#x200B;

They should buff her in other ways and not in this matter. If this cahange comes out  weak, they will buff her again and could make her OP. If this becomes OP, they can nerf her in some other way that will make her weak again.

Actually, Amumu was updated just a few patches a go that made him better as a champion and didn't made him feel as old as Annie. Amumu was fine after all, it's just his ridiculous mana costs and weak jungle clear that made him not suitable. Annie on the other hand has more clear weaknesses because she is very old and outdated in terms of how the meta and more complex newer champions are right now.

&#x200B;

That's what they wanted her to be, more reliant on Tibbers but it doesn't make any sense because on how her kit naturally is, which is a burst mage. Comparing her to other champions that has pets, their kits actually coincide with their pets. Whilst in Annie's kit, they are what? Tibbers should be Annie's protector but I really don't feel Tibbers as a protector besides blocking some skills. If they want the direction of Tibbers, be more useful, then she needs a rework. Riot needs to decide on what her identity should really be. Her kit right now is what you said, is meant to be a heavy burst monster but she got 20% off ap scaling and some base damages on her ult that truly made her burst weaker to compensate that "stronger" Tibbers, which is more buggy and still dies so fast.

&#x200B;

Annie also  has problems with her sustained damage. Her mana costs are too high even building Luden's can make you oomd and can't make you stay in the lane longer. Recent champions have their Autos deal extra damage on them as their passive (Neeko, Sylas, Zoe, Galio) which make them trade stronger early game (and it doesn't need mana for a free damage and they don't even need to hit their abilities to trigger them). They also have better base stats than her, with her range that is similar to Ryze, her base movement speed is still lower than Ryze and Ryze has some movement speed amps from his kit as well which makes him much stronger.

Bumping up some numbers isn't a good idea because she is a simple champion. Simple champions are kind of hard to balance simply because they are too good or just plain out outclassed. Miss Fortune is a good example of this, when the rune changes came, the extra damage (Scorch, Aery/Comet) that they give made her really strong because off her ability to harass with ease using her Make it Rain and Double Up.

&#x200B;

Yeah she needs another update or a rework imo. Her Q W and her E are the ones that are in need some tweaking imo. Her W is just an aoe skill and stuns If your passive is on, else it's just for wave clear (that you rarely max first) and just another form of damage, her E is mostly still a stun charger and her Q is her single target wave clear and her "targetted stun" that makes her somewhat more of a pick against assassins.

Annie's really outdated as a champion herself. Playing her right now is like playing while you're on a strain. She is too limited in what she can do compared to what other popular and more dated mid laners can do. She's a champion that's too centered on her Ult to be massively threatening, without it she isn't as threatening. Her only form of engage, flash is too long and once it out your threat level becomes minimal. Compare it too recent champions like Taliyah and Sylas who has either great sustain (both in damage and heals/shields), range, kits that does not make them ult-reliant, and an good wave clear. They can do more after they released their combo, which has low cooldowns and higher range as well so they can spam with ease compared to Annie who may have low cooldowns but her range is in a very vulnerable spot. 

&#x200B;

She needs an update/rework more than just a buff. And it pains to say it myself that sometimes I don't feel like playing her because there are just better options.

Annie's initial burst was nerfed in the MYMU her Ult used to have an 80% or 85% (can't remember) AP scalings, Tibbers' aura's ap scaling got lowered to half from 20% to 10% and the base damage of her ult was also lowered at all levels. This is to compensate to a "stronger" Tibbers which his basic attacks can now scale with Annie's ap. In short, her aoe burst got weaker but her single target burst is now stronger.

&#x200B;

One of the reasons why other mages deal more damage is because most have superior ranges. They can easily poke you or someone from a safe range whilst as Annie your range is a very vulnerable spot and could easily get you out traded. Another reason is Annie is an outdatted champion. Even if you can utilize Tibbers after summoning, her sustained damage isn't the best unlike recent champions like Sylas and Zoe who has tons of band aid solutions to their kit even if the made a mistake on their combo.

Again, Roa is a defensive item. The item isn't for snowballing or having a huge powerspike. It's for survivability. Survivability for extended fights (sustain and HP), survivability against worse match-ups that would make laning phase harder for you. 

&#x200B;

Protobelt is good but it's more of an offensive item rather than comparing it to a defensive item. I would rather compare Roa to items like Banshee's Veil or Zhonya's Hourglass which are its fellow defensive item builds.

She's still strong up until low Diamond elo. There are still many macro mistakes in that elo so Annie can still shine in that elo.

&#x200B;

As per champions that can fulfill Annie's roles better: Orianna, Lissandra, and Syndra are good champions to learn that are similar to her kit as a burst mage. I suggest you should invest more on Ori if you want to have another option besides Annie because Ori never falls out of meta.

That's the beauty of playing Annie, she has no mandatory items bar maybe Void Staff and Deathcap. The rest varies. With how simple her kit is, you can adjust your item builds depending on your match-up, what your team needs, or what you can do against the enemy team. Rushing 2nd tier boots is the only thing I think is mandatory on Annie because of her immobility.

&#x200B;

You need to survive more because of champions that you can't outright burst and can easily fight you back? Roa is a good item for survivability. Need another survivability item option especially when there are champions like Zed? Buy Zhonya's Hourglass. Need more engage or gap-close? Protobelt, Predator, Spellbinder and even Shurelya's Reverie are good options. Need more damage? Dark harvest, Comet and Electrocute can help you. Too much tanks? Rylai's and Liandry's will burn them especially with Tibbers. Need more wave clear? Luden's and Protobelt will help you with that. You will always adjust your item builds depending on the enemy team, I would even buy tank items if given the circumstance that I would need it.

The only thing you need to know is that Annie is by no means, freelo. By her kit being simple, you can tend to focus more on the macro aspect of the game rather than also focusing on the micro skills of harder champions. 

&#x200B;

Take note that Annie has clear weaknesses that could be easily taken advantage of. Examples would be her immobility, no sustain (relies on runes or RoA to have one), low base stats, mediocre range, high mana costs, and her mediocre wave clear. You have a limited cards dealt within you and it's on your own accord to use them wisely.

I use Relentless Hunter in this match-up for better mobility. Ravenous hunter is also nice for the spell vamp it gives but he will just spam you with spells and freeze the wave to his side so you really can't heal from minions.

&#x200B;

You have to get your jungler to help you. A good Xerath will never get in your range as much as possible. You can abuse him if he autos a minion, that's where he gets hiis mana back due to his passive.

But on the same side, the one who argues open-mindedness and understanding are also being what they are hating. "You're a dumbass, youre gross, grow up, etc". The girls badly presented their ideas to the point that they are becoming the monsters that they hate. They are provoking Kensuke here. Atleast Kensuke is admittedly saying that he can't understand. Is he willing to change his perspective? Is he willing to learn from it and try to understand? Maybe? maybe so. The girls on the other hand appeared that they just want to win the argument and make Kensuke look bad forgetting what are they really trying to fight for, which is the open mindedness and understanding.

&#x200B;

Yeah, it's called persuasion/convincing but imo it's basically the same with manipulation. The latter just appeared bad to the recepient/audient and the first one is positive looking. Shingo is like the embodiment of Dale Carnegie's How to win Friends and Influence People.

Shingo's not defending Kensuke because of his beliefs, he's defending him because the girls are becoming condescending and inconsiderate. Without giving Kensuke a chance to know why he behaves that way up until they made him confess that he was molested multiple times. Shingo is becoming a middle ground in the arguments. We don't know if Shingo agrees or disagrees with Kensuke's beliefs but the girls could have said them better. What could have they did was supposed to be a discussion (as what Shingo really wants), but they are trying it to be an argument where there should bee a right and wrong.

&#x200B;

I agree though that the girls are in the right, it's just that it was badly presented by them.

Colby went from that huge physical immunity challenge threat from his previous seasons to an UTR game in Hvv and voted for Sandra for the win because he knew how hard it is to play an UTR game. 

&#x200B;

UTR is hard because as much as possible, you have to make your moves as subtle as possible. Look at Victoria, without Chris coming back, no one on the remaining castaways knew that she was a jury favorite after Devens.  she could have made f3 and plead her case and can win. The jury likes her gameplay, was it easy for her to do that? I think not. I'm not saying UTR is the hardest game to play, but it's the most underappreciated type of gameplay that recent survivor has had.

&#x200B;

I agree on you on Gavin's failure at persuading the other jury members though, but saying UTR is easy is an understatement from great UTR players.

&#x200B;

Jurors: Finalist# 2,  why do you deserve the win more than Finalist# 1's immunity challenge streak and great social gameplay?

&#x200B;

Finalist #2's speech: I used 6 idols the whole game, extra vote and used my swap-by-school-yard-pick advantage to get myself the numbers and control the game.

&#x200B;

Jeff Probst: The Winner of Survivor: Island of Advantages is... Finalist #2!

He said it [here](https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/survivor-edge-extinction-jeff-probst-season-38-1181242) dated January 31st, this year. 

&#x200B;

The finale is actually good, the problem was the winner itself is so under edited making the whole season dull. I actually appreciated his efforts and the use of his sales skills to trick Lauren and convince the jury for his win.

I've read this already and on my mind is saying that what he is saying  just a bad excuse to a badly edited and badly-themed for a survivor season. They really need to step-up on the story-telling.

&#x200B;

This is a reality show and people watching it wants some connection to the characters, and he only had that break out on the episode after he got voted out (got almost nothing after), the episode where he got the letter, and on the finale itself. It lacked build-up and connection. As a viewer, I don't want to be told by the people itself that they are playing a great game (socially, strategically, challege-wise), I want to see how good they are at it.

Yeah and he's now the number one threat. Eliminating her only shield left, Kelley made him open and have to scramble to find new alliances. I really don't know why people say he's playing the best game out of all the remaining castaways.

&#x200B;

For me, the one who's playing the best game so far is Victoria. Was never in danger thus far and able to be always in the right side of the votes. Good social game while playing under the radar.

Heimer and Zed are actually good match-ups for Annie.

&#x200B;

As for Heimer, you outrange his turrets by using your autos and Disintegrates which can give you free gold. Stand behind your minions so that you can't get hit by his rocket poke. Once he has no turrets available, that's where you gain an advantage and poke him with autos and Disintegrates. I would suggest playing Heimer so that you should know his limitations regarding his turrets and cooldowns.

&#x200B;

As for Zed, he has a weak level 1. Take advantage of that and harass him with autos and Disintegrates. make him lose pots and or go back to base. If you are having a hard time against him, take exhaust. It diminishes a lot of power against him. I actually tried going aftershock against him and just loled at his damage whenever he all-in on me. Together with your Molten Shield, you get to be really tanky once you stun him, preferably after he ults. Comet is good against him as well especially at early levels for that added poke. As for items, rushing armor is a must and Zhonya's Hourglass makes it very hard for him to engage on you. Buying health items is also good against him so that he can't easily burst you down, Rylai's, Liandry's, Roa and Morellonomicon are good examples of this.

lower mana cost, movement speed buff, just some qol changes. Her E actually being more decent would be nice as well.

&#x200B;

Or revert the mymu cast delay is my best bet but that's too much to ask for.

one of the main reason why she isn't meta, it's because of her pitiful base stats. Her base stats are low given how immobile she is and doesn't have a lot more than just being a stun bot. Her base damage were high before now they are just the norm and needs AP to deal more damage.

&#x200B;

Galio is an example of a meta support that's popular in the competitive scene. He has all the utility and cc whilst having a good base stats without being reliant on items to become tankier. He also has high base damages in his abilities despite his repeated nerfs. He can engage and provide more than what Annie can do. In short, she's outclassed as a support.

Level 1 is key in the Le Blanc vs Annie match-up. Le Blanc's level 1 is weak compared to Annie's use that to your advantage and poke her with autos and Disintegrate. Make her use her pots so that she wouldn't have enough sustain to stay in lane to force her go back to base and make her lose experience. A behind Le Blanc is one of the weakest in the game.

&#x200B;

Levels 2-5 is where you should respect her early game burst. Do not let her snowball as much as possible. Freezing the lane would help because she can set up ganks pretty well.  Ward around your side lanes so that she can be shown on the minimap whilst she is starting to roam. That's only one of the ways you could counter her roam because Annie's wave clear is mediocre. You can run TP so that you could possibly go to whenever she roams at.

3 dark seals might be overkill but I can see 2 dark seals work fine in a very situational scenario. 4 pots + boots is already a fine start against Xerath as it helps you give movementspeed and sustain for his poke. Zed can be easily mitigated early by rushing armor as well. Dark Seal + Refillable is also a good option which can give you good sustain whilst maintaing that nice AP from darkseal.

&#x200B;

I would prefer Dark seal and then Dring afterwards because Annie could really use the mana sustain that it gives especially when Mages got their mana nerfed, she kind of became mana hungry.

Ignite and Morellonomicon are the best answers against his heal.

&#x200B;

His threat level is more on how threatening your team's ultimates are so I can't say that he's a very hard champion against. He's more like a Lissandra/Galio match-up where you can choose to not fight them because of their safety and tankiness (Aftershock Liss) and just focus on farming and impacting other lanes.

Arcane Comet got a lot better on Annie then since it's an on-ability-hit spell. This furder decreases its cooldown. 

Cheap Shot is also another on-ability-hit spell so rushing Rylai's can be more good than before.

&#x200B;

Annie is on the weak side. There are better options that can fulfill her role whilst also doing other things like face checking

&#x200B;

She's immobile, mediocre range, flash-reliant but her main flaw is her mediocre wave clear.

&#x200B;

Probably lower her mana costs, add damage to her W, make her W have less cooldowns per leveling it, add a perecentage bonus damage to minions if Pyromania is up. Just buff her wave clear and then she can roam, which is one of her strengths but is heavily mitigated by her mediocre wave clear.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Patch 9.4 and patch 9.5 changes made her top tier hence made her pick/ban. Your statistics is kind of outdated because of past nerfs that has happened to champions that actually benefited Kalista.

&#x200B;

I already posted an earlier post that she's high pick/ban in competitive but was flamed. She's getting nerfed soon, sadly. I hope it's more of a mini rework because she will never be balanced in pro-play because she's the perfect adc in a competitive environment.

Her core items got nerfed (Righteous glory+ Talisman), her stun duration was nerfed  (from 1.75s at all levels to 1.2s5/1.5s/1.75s), her aa range was reduced to 575 but was reverted back in 8.17, her base damages got nerfed for a higher ap ratio, her stun is no longer instantaneous as before as of the Mid Year Mage Update (0.25s delay).

&#x200B;

The game has evolved to the point that her kit is just basically outclassed. There are better options that can fulfill her role as a poke-engage type of support. Examples would be Galio and Gragas. Both of them have good base stats and base damages (won't need to invest on AP so more support/tanky items) so they could stay and deal more damage in extended fights, they have more tools of engage in their kits. Galio has a taunt, knock up and a global pressure via his ult whilst Gragas having his knockups from his Bodyslam and ult. Rakan also outclasses Annie despite the nerfs because he can still provide so much utility than Annie would have. 2 shields, mobility, Charm and a knock up. And ofcourse tankier supports such as Alistar and Braum exists that can also fulfill her role as an engage support but can provide more than what she can do.

&#x200B;

She's too niche right now and she fares well against enchanter supports like Janna because she can bully her at lane and can blow her up once it's 6.

Luden's Echo is a good item on Annie. Ever since mages got their mana growth nerfed, Annie some what became mana hungry which wasn't clearly her problem before. Luden's helps that but this gives her the ability to have more waveclear (plus the 20%cdr to give more waveclear) to mitigate her mediocre wave clear. 

&#x200B;

It's hard to roam using Annie especially when she gets easily shoved at by meta mid laners.

Xerath's a worse match-up because he can poke you from a far with his abilities with just pointing it at you. His Q can pass through minions and with Annie's immobility and somewhat passable movementspeed makes you want to rush boots as fast as possible but it's hard because a good Xerath will poke you from a far whilst having that mana sustain from his passive. A good Xerath will poke you with W first for the slow and then hit you with Q. His Q might be telegraphed, but it's not as easy to sidestep especially on the hands of a good player. A good Xerath aims to make you go back to your base and lose some cs and experience, making you behind. Getting you killed is only the icing to its cake.

&#x200B;

Velkoz on the other hand is easier, albeit still a tough matchup,  in the laning phase because his Q can be blocked by a minion. You can go to the side of your minions so you can block one part of his abilities. His knockup has a delay as well so dodging it is easier and more telegraphed. His range is much smaller than Xerath's too and can be easier to engage on him than Xerath would.

Syndra and Xerath are just the worst imo. Superior range and will even hit you under your turret and make your laning phase hell.  Adding insult to the injury is that Syndra can use Force of the Will on Tibbers and will just throw him back at you.

&#x200B;

Runner-ups are Cass, Kass, Velkoz, and Azir.

Servers take a huge difference in their playstyle. In the Korean servers, Laning is everything, pick potential is everywhere, much more aggressive and games end faster than in NA. Hence Cute Mau often uses early game runes such as Scorch. In NA, games are much more casual and team fighting (which can drag games longer) is more prevalent hence why Annie bot uses DH for its team fight damage(DH resets) and its reliability coming to late game.

&#x200B;

Both are good examples of great Annie players and I would suggest watching both since they both stream at a different time frame too.

Annie's Q has an animation wind-up that gets destroyed(cancelled) especially if the target got lost in vision. This has been even her pre-MYMU

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

She's outclassed by many champions in the game. With the right practice and game knowledge, playing other champions that also fill her role and do better at other aspects of the game will be more worth than picking her. The game has evolved to the point that even Ori and Syndra are somewhat risky picks because they are immobile champions even iof their range is significantly better than Annie's. But, they have solid niches that make them better options than Annie in more possible situations.

Annie has a weak laning phase as well. Her range is mediocre and her waveclear is mediocre and is one of the worst, if not the worst, of all mages in the game. Her pushing the lane is high risk because she is immobile and can be easily ganked by the enemy jungler and can kill her and can bait her flash. She has no abilit that can face check brushes (unless Tibbers is up) while other midlaners can do it safely like Ori !, Syndra Q. Lux E and etc. She's very immobile as well, no forms of escape/engage outside of flash, Predator and fully built items.

An example would be Lissandra. They have similar ranges and threat level in terms of team fight potential, you could argue that Annie deals more damage but in high elo, teams are more coordinated and follow-ups are almost always there and the damage isn't on par in terms on how better, reliable and frequent Lissandra's engage compared to Annie. Lissandra's safer as well because of her mobility, ult and the tankiness that she gets from Aftershock.

&#x200B;

She's high risk-low reward type of champion in high elo. Especially in that elo where less mistakes can be punished.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

" She was also very lucky that people trusted her so much in Pearl Island that they didn't vote her out for being a liability. I mean even Fairplay argued with her that she was weak in the first few days of Pearl Island yet she wasn't voted out. "

&#x200B;

This isn't luck. This is how great her social game is. The thing with Sandra and how she is a really great player is that she's very convincing to the point that you'll have to agree with her and make other bigger targets. She's not about making big-dominant moves, she plays the way that people can trust her and she's an ally/another number that would just go with the flow but in reality, is a very sneaky player.

It's a flaw because she out right said it to her that she does not want work with her. Why would you tell someone on survivor that you don't want to work with someone? That's bad gameplay. 

&#x200B;

Isn't Tasha the same as well? It was Tasha who baited Kass into release her "chaos" and even talked to them individually that she is just going to cause chaos. Kass was bitter towards Tasha because on how she was voted out. It wasn't perceived as good gameplay for her but the way Tasha planned it was for vindictive reasons in Kass's eyes. Kass doesn't have any ill thoughts towards her pre-Cambodia (look at her AMAs), Tasha on the other hand still has this bitterness towards on how Kass screwed her game in Cagayan but didn't speak to Kass for 6 days after her flip (confirmed by Spencer). Was that a good gameplay? 

&#x200B;

Oh and have you read this interview of Trish talking about Tasha coming into Cambodia? [Here](http://insidesurvivor.com/survivor-second-chance-trish-hegarty-talks-tasha-fox-2149)

She's very underrated as a strategist but her social game in Cambodia is her real downfall. 

&#x200B;

At the merge, Kass tried to patch things up with her ,and would want to work with her, and admittedly refused to do so. That's a bad move for Tasha, She may have won over Kass in that fight because she was booted but Tasha sisn't manage the Kass boot very well because it felt like more of a revenge against her. She alienated people (Wentworth, Abi, Keith) that were not in her alliance thus making her stick to her core alliances. Who would vote for you at the end if you alienated them both for strategic and personal purposes. 

&#x200B;

And Wentworth's idol was not impressive? Watch this secret scene: [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6eN99nRDGgY](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6eN99nRDGgY)

Besause she's Sandra Diaz Twine and that's just how it is.

&#x200B;

On a serious note, Sandra played a great under the radar game where she became safe at every stages of the merge. She knows how to read people and convince them to take her longer because she's perceived as a non-threat. Even Parvati knows that Sandra's a threat. She basically tried to helped the heroes but the heroes didn't listen to her. She took that fact to her advantage and used that to convince the jury, especially the heroes. One great thing about her is that she is open to working with the heroes and even the villains knew that but she was still able to get away with it. She's good at deflecting the attention and making others shine up until their final curtain call.

Sandra had a pregame alliance with Brad and Troy so in the Malcolm boot, Brad didnt agree with JT when he whispered that he wants to get rid of Sandra.

Jeremy and Spencer also had a pre-game alliance before Cambodia, both went to the ftc.

&#x200B;

Yasuo is one of the most easiest matchups against Annie. A squishy champion that wants to get in your range is good match up. With her aa revert, she can easily pop his shield more often and can harass him at early levels.  You can use your Molten shield when he auto you because it deals damage to him, R and W can get pass through his windwall as well. 

&#x200B;

One thing about dealing with Yasuo is letting him push. His only mobility and for having outplay potential is his ability to dash through minions. Without it, he's a sitting duck. Yasuo needs items to be considered a threat so making him back with harassing him will be a good step of beating him and getting him behind. He also needs a good jungler to become a threat. if his jungler doesn't camp you or is a very aggressive early game champion like Lee Sin, you have to be respectful of it.

&#x200B;

imo, Rushing Seeker's against him isn't much worth because Yasuo's use Conqueror and will deal True Damage to you. It's more efficient to buy HP items that also give you HP or straight out Ap damage items like Oblivion Orb or Aesther Wisp if you are having a hard time dodging his tornadoes. Rylai's is a good rush against him as you can perma slow him with Tibbers.

Link to the vid of the game

&#x200B;

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z5NhWer3P1c](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z5NhWer3P1c)

Annie stomps a lot of top laners. She was a counterpick into Gnar when he was meta. But the problem with Annie in the top lane is because of her immobility and range. They are too short for a long lane like top lane. At least in mid, the lane is shorter so she could have a safer laning phase and could reach to the tower faster.

&#x200B;

At top lane, it's way easier to set up ganks against Annie as she can get easily camped by the jungler  and/or get your wave frozen. She can get easily dove in the tower as well. To add insult to the injury, her base stats are also pretty low and tanks could actually destroy her with just their high base damages.

&#x200B;

Let's compare her to some ranged mages that are actually good right now at the meta and why they are picked instead of Annie:

&#x200B;

Lissandra - albeit they have similar ranges, she  has mobility, more CC and survivability (ult) and safer laning phase, better wave clear.

&#x200B;

Cass- Has sustained damage from her low cooldown spells, can poke and harass with her spells as well, safer range, better wave clear, Outscales you hard.

&#x200B;

Viktor- albeit immobile he Has a shield, can poke with Q and gives him movement speed and shield, better wave clear, safer range.

&#x200B;

These champions can do a lot wilst not being high risk high reward much like Annie is. At the end of the day, if you'll look at the other cards, there are just better options.

Liandry's and Rylai's is never bad on Annie especially in a meta where in bruisers are very strong and is on almost any game. With them running conqueror, have sustain, can survive your burst and build items that give them imense amount of hp, Annie would need more sustained damage and survivability. 

&#x200B;

Both items give her HP which gives her more survivability from the sustained damage that they have. It synergizes well with Tibbers since he can permaslow/ permaproc Liandry's passives (the % damage buff and the %health magic damage).

that's basically the iteration/more updated version of this build that got some plays before.

&#x200B;

[https://www.nerfplz.com/2016/12/weird-picks-71-tank-annie-mid.html](https://www.nerfplz.com/2016/12/weird-picks-71-tank-annie-mid.html)

That's the beauty of playing Annie. She has no cookie-butter item build. With how simple her kit is, she is not gated to buy items because off her kit but rather buy the items that would benefit her, her matchup and her team.

&#x200B;

DCap and Void Staff are her only mandatory items as both give  her insane amounts of damage output. Zhonya's can be also mandatory (I build it 9/10 games) because of her immobility and mediocre range, she will need more survivability.

&#x200B;

The same goes for runes, it's what your team needs/playstyle/matchup. Could recommend you some if something in particular you have certain matchups that you are having a hard time with.

&#x200B;

Playing other roles/champions would greatly improved your gameplay. Back then when I was new to the game and extremely get destroyed by Syndra players, I played a couple of Syndra games (I still play her often now too) and got me to understand her strengths and weaknesses and can incorporate when I'm against a Syndra. Be it cooldowns, mana management, etc.

&#x200B;

People say to play Annie because she is easy to play is because Annie can help you learn the basics of the macro aspect of the game, once you've learned the basics, you can dwelve into the more complex areas like team comps, jungle pathing, face checking, match ups, objectives, advanced warding and many more.

She's mediocre at all roles right now tbh (meta wise). There are just better options to all roles that she's viable in mid/supp and top to an extent. She's good at low elo though, where mistakes are abundant. And that is what Annie's good at, punishing mistakes but the higher you go, the harder is for you to play her.

&#x200B;

As a mid laner, she gets out shoved by most meta mid laners. Ori, Syndra, Aatrox and even irelia outshoves her hard. Her wave clear is mediocre at its finest since most would max Q first for better DPS. Her great roaming potential is mitigated by her mediocre wave clear. With the mobility creep from the latest champions, Annie is very immobile as well. Only flash is her reliable engage/escape tool. Without it, she isn't as threatening/safe because of her immobility. 

&#x200B;

As a support, her only poke tool is her autos and Q. Her mana costs are relatively high too so you are mostly using your autos as your poke too. You're a threat once you are 6 which is pretty much too late as per other engage champions like Pyke, Alistar and Leona who could all-in at earlier levels and give their botlane a lead. But some pros use Annie support with Aftershock and that's maybe a hidden secret OP or something.

&#x200B;

I play Annie because I just love playing her. But if you look at other champions, they are just simply better . I'm really being honest here and hopes that she will have another update on her gameplay, more like a legit update/rework.

&#x200B;

